{"name":"Golden Brown","hex":"#b27a01","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"golden brown","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Brown with golden highlights."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A yellowish orangey color; a brown color with a golden tinge."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/golden_brown"}],"entries":[{"title":"Golden Brown","blocks":[{"text":"\"Golden Brown\" is a song by the English rock band the Stranglers, released as a 7-inch single on EMI's Liberty label in 1982. Noted for its distinctive harpsichord instrumentation, it was the second single released from the band's sixth studio album La Folie (1981). The single peaked at No. 2 on the UK Singles Chart, making it the band's highest-charting single in the country."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Golden_Brown.png/330px-Golden_Brown.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_Brown","wikidata":"Q1534853"},{"title":"Brown","blocks":[{"text":"Brown is a color. It can be thought as a darker, typically desaturated shade of orange, and can often be produced by combining red and yellow with another color, namely blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg/330px-Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brown","wikidata":"Q47071"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/cf/The_Stranglers_-_